<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-images-as-matrices" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Digital Images as Matrices</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Every operation in this chapter was introduced through its arithmetic. In this
      section we look at the same operations through a different window: a digital
      photograph <em>is</em> a matrix, so each operation we have defined does something
      visible to a picture. Scaling a matrix changes the brightness, transposing it
      reflects the picture across a diagonal, multiplying by a suitable matrix flips or
      crops it, and multiplying by an inverse undoes a scramble. Nothing new is
      introduced here; the point is that the arithmetic already developed has content
      you can see.
    </p>
    <p>
      Interactive <term>Sage cells</term> are scattered throughout. Press
      <term>Evaluate</term> to run one in your browser, or copy the code into
      <url href="https://sagecell.sagemath.org" visual="sagecell.sagemath.org">the Sage
      cell server</url>. The cells on this page share their variables, but they must be
      evaluated in order, so run each one as you reach it.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-images-grayscale">
    <title>A Photograph Is a Matrix</title>

    <p>
      A digital image is a rectangular grid of <term>pixels</term>. A colour image
      records three numbers at each pixel <mdash /> the intensities of red, green, and
      blue <mdash /> so an image that is <m>m</m> pixels tall and <m>n</m> pixels wide
      is stored as three <m>m \times n</m> matrices <m>R</m>, <m>G</m>, and <m>B</m>.
      Each entry is an integer between <m>0</m> and <m>255</m>, where <m>0</m> means
      none of that colour and <m>255</m> means as much as the display can produce.
    </p>

    <p>
      To convert the image to grayscale we must replace the three numbers at each pixel
      by a single number recording how bright that pixel is. The standard choice is the
      weighted average
      <md>
        M = 0.299\,R + 0.587\,G + 0.114\,B,
      </md>
      with the resulting entries rounded to integers. Read that formula again with the
      chapter in mind: it is a linear combination of three matrices, built from exactly
      the scalar multiplication and addition of
      <xref ref="sec-matrix-operations" text="title" />. The weights are
      unequal because the human eye is far more sensitive to green light than to blue,
      so an unweighted average <m>\frac{1}{3}(R + G + B)</m> would render greens too dark
      and blues too light.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-sca-gray">
      <caption>
        A colour image (a) and its grayscale version (b), obtained by forming the linear
        combination <m>M = 0.299\,R + 0.587\,G + 0.114\,B</m> of the three colour
        matrices. From here on we work with the single matrix <m>M</m> in (b).
      </caption>
      <sidebyside widths="44% 44%" margins="auto">
        <figure xml:id="fig-sca-colour">
          <caption>The colour image, stored as three matrices.</caption>
          <image source="images/sca-colour.png">
            <shortdescription>An illustration of a cat sitting inside a wooden box labelled Schrodinger's Cat, beside a mechanism connected to a container marked with a skull and crossbones</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-sca-grayscale">
          <caption>The grayscale image, stored as the single matrix <m>M</m>.</caption>
          <image source="images/sca-gray.png">
            <shortdescription>The same illustration in shades of gray</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The grayscale image in <xref ref="fig-sca-grayscale" text="global" /> is
      <m>780</m> pixels tall and <m>520</m> pixels wide, so <m>M</m> is a
      <m>780 \times 520</m> matrix with <m>405{,}600</m> entries
      <mdash /> far too large to print. But nothing stops us from looking at a piece of
      it. A <term>submatrix</term> of <m>M</m>, obtained by keeping some of its rows and
      some of its columns, is exactly a rectangular patch of the picture. The patch
      around the cat's left eye <mdash /> rows <m>297</m> through <m>376</m> and columns
      <m>231</m> through <m>290</m> <mdash /> is an <m>80 \times 60</m> submatrix, which
      is still <m>4800</m> numbers. So we shrink it: cut the patch into
      <m>5 \times 5</m> blocks of pixels and replace each block by the average of its
      <m>25</m> entries. Averaging blocks like this is how an image is reduced to a
      lower resolution, and it leaves the <m>16 \times 12</m> matrix
      <md>
        D = \begin{bmatrix}
             74 \amp  66 \amp  53 \amp  52 \amp  73 \amp  78 \amp  80 \amp  82 \amp  82 \amp  78 \amp  82 \amp  77 \\
             62 \amp  56 \amp  65 \amp  84 \amp  83 \amp  87 \amp  83 \amp  89 \amp  84 \amp  80 \amp  84 \amp  74 \\
             73 \amp  67 \amp  84 \amp  87 \amp  87 \amp  89 \amp  92 \amp  89 \amp  91 \amp  86 \amp  89 \amp  81 \\
             70 \amp  80 \amp  85 \amp  87 \amp  91 \amp  91 \amp  91 \amp  82 \amp  94 \amp  90 \amp  91 \amp  86 \\
             67 \amp  84 \amp  87 \amp  92 \amp  97 \amp  95 \amp  95 \amp  82 \amp  95 \amp  91 \amp  85 \amp  86 \\
             83 \amp  90 \amp  92 \amp  87 \amp  75 \amp  76 \amp  91 \amp  93 \amp  95 \amp  91 \amp  86 \amp  86 \\
             90 \amp  90 \amp  80 \amp  47 \amp  70 \amp  75 \amp  55 \amp  87 \amp  95 \amp  93 \amp  88 \amp  90 \\
             87 \amp  88 \amp  54 \amp  89 \amp 123 \amp  36 \amp  76 \amp  59 \amp  96 \amp  95 \amp  94 \amp  91 \\
             95 \amp 106 \amp  60 \amp 116 \amp 103 \amp  27 \amp  76 \amp  74 \amp  92 \amp  94 \amp  96 \amp  89 \\
             99 \amp 111 \amp  81 \amp 108 \amp 133 \amp  49 \amp 120 \amp  78 \amp  84 \amp  95 \amp  94 \amp  86 \\
            101 \amp 107 \amp 109 \amp  72 \amp 115 \amp 132 \amp 104 \amp  48 \amp  88 \amp 102 \amp 101 \amp  83 \\
             93 \amp 100 \amp 104 \amp 111 \amp  97 \amp  86 \amp  94 \amp  86 \amp  99 \amp 106 \amp 105 \amp  90 \\
             91 \amp  94 \amp  97 \amp 102 \amp 101 \amp  99 \amp 104 \amp  95 \amp 118 \amp 120 \amp 111 \amp  91 \\
             84 \amp  91 \amp  91 \amp  98 \amp 105 \amp 110 \amp 116 \amp  85 \amp  85 \amp  83 \amp  78 \amp  62 \\
             79 \amp  94 \amp  98 \amp 100 \amp 103 \amp 115 \amp 118 \amp  79 \amp  60 \amp  79 \amp  57 \amp  51 \\
             79 \amp  84 \amp  92 \amp  85 \amp 109 \amp 113 \amp 116 \amp 116 \amp  76 \amp  64 \amp  53 \amp  90
        \end{bmatrix}
      </md>
      shown magnified in <xref ref="fig-sca-detail" text="global" />. Each entry is now
      one square of the picture: the small numbers are the dark squares and the large
      ones are the light squares. The <m>27</m> in row <m>9</m> is the darkest part of
      the pupil, and the <m>133</m> in the row below it is the brightest.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-sca-detail">
      <caption>
        The <m>16 \times 12</m> matrix <m>D</m>, drawn as an image and magnified so that
        the individual squares are visible. Every square is one entry of <m>D</m>, and
        every entry is the average of a <m>5 \times 5</m> block of pixels from around
        the cat's left eye; <m>0</m> would be black and <m>255</m> would be white.
      </caption>
      <image source="images/sca-detail.png" width="34%">
        <shortdescription>A coarse grid of gray squares, each square one entry of the matrix D</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Let us confirm in Sage that these numbers really do produce that picture. The
      command <c>matrix_plot</c> draws one square per entry, and the option
      <c>cmap='gray'</c> asks for the shading we have just described. The option
      <c>vmin=0, vmax=255</c> pins the ends of the scale to black and white, so that a
      given number always produces the same shade.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        D = matrix(ZZ, [
        [ 74,  66,  53,  52,  73,  78,  80,  82,  82,  78,  82,  77],
        [ 62,  56,  65,  84,  83,  87,  83,  89,  84,  80,  84,  74],
        [ 73,  67,  84,  87,  87,  89,  92,  89,  91,  86,  89,  81],
        [ 70,  80,  85,  87,  91,  91,  91,  82,  94,  90,  91,  86],
        [ 67,  84,  87,  92,  97,  95,  95,  82,  95,  91,  85,  86],
        [ 83,  90,  92,  87,  75,  76,  91,  93,  95,  91,  86,  86],
        [ 90,  90,  80,  47,  70,  75,  55,  87,  95,  93,  88,  90],
        [ 87,  88,  54,  89, 123,  36,  76,  59,  96,  95,  94,  91],
        [ 95, 106,  60, 116, 103,  27,  76,  74,  92,  94,  96,  89],
        [ 99, 111,  81, 108, 133,  49, 120,  78,  84,  95,  94,  86],
        [101, 107, 109,  72, 115, 132, 104,  48,  88, 102, 101,  83],
        [ 93, 100, 104, 111,  97,  86,  94,  86,  99, 106, 105,  90],
        [ 91,  94,  97, 102, 101,  99, 104,  95, 118, 120, 111,  91],
        [ 84,  91,  91,  98, 105, 110, 116,  85,  85,  83,  78,  62],
        [ 79,  94,  98, 100, 103, 115, 118,  79,  60,  79,  57,  51],
        [ 79,  84,  92,  85, 109, 113, 116, 116,  76,  64,  53,  90]])
        matrix_plot(D, cmap='gray', vmin=0, vmax=255)
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      Change one entry to <c>0</c> and evaluate the cell again: a single square turns
      black. The matrix and the picture are the same object.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-images-scalar-addition">
    <title>Scalar Multiplication and Addition: Brightness and Negatives</title>

    <p>
      Multiplying <m>M</m> by a scalar <m>c > 1</m> raises every intensity by the same
      factor and so brightens the picture, while <m>0 \lt c \lt 1</m> darkens it. There
      is a wrinkle: an intensity above <m>255</m> is not a colour any display can show,
      so after computing <m>cM</m> the values above <m>255</m> must be replaced by
      <m>255</m>. That last step is <em>not</em> a matrix operation, and it destroys
      information <mdash /> several distinct bright values collapse to the same white,
      which is why an overexposed photograph cannot be repaired by darkening it.
    </p>

    <p>
      Addition is just as visible. Let <m>J</m> be the <m>m \times n</m> matrix all of
      whose entries are <m>1</m>, so that <m>255J</m> is a completely white image. Then
      <md>
        N = 255J - M
      </md>
      replaces each intensity <m>v</m> by <m>255 - v</m>, turning black into white and
      white into black. This is the photographic <term>negative</term>, and it needs no
      clipping: if <m>0 \leq v \leq 255</m> then <m>0 \leq 255 - v \leq 255</m>
      automatically.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-sca-negative">
      <caption>
        Scalar multiplication and addition applied to <m>M</m>. In (a) every entry is
        multiplied by <m>1.4</m> and anything exceeding <m>255</m> is clipped; in (b)
        every entry <m>v</m> is replaced by <m>255 - v</m>.
      </caption>
      <sidebyside widths="44% 44%" margins="auto">
        <figure xml:id="fig-sca-brighter">
          <caption>Brightened: <m>1.4\,M</m>, clipped at <m>255</m>.</caption>
          <image source="images/sca-brighter.png">
            <shortdescription>The grayscale illustration, noticeably lighter, with the brightest areas washed out to white</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-sca-negative-image">
          <caption>The negative: <m>255J - M</m>.</caption>
          <image source="images/sca-negative.png">
            <shortdescription>The grayscale illustration with light and dark reversed, like a photographic negative</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Both operations are worth trying on the submatrix <m>D</m>. In Sage,
      <c>D.nrows()</c> and <c>D.ncols()</c> report the dimensions of <m>D</m>, and
      <c>ones_matrix</c> builds the matrix <m>J</m> of that same size.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        m, n = D.nrows(), D.ncols()
        J = ones_matrix(ZZ, m, n)
        N = 255*J - D
        graphics_array([matrix_plot(D, cmap='gray', vmin=0, vmax=255),
                        matrix_plot(N, cmap='gray', vmin=0, vmax=255)]).show()
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      Applying the operation twice returns the original, since
      <m>255J - (255J - M) = M</m>. Replace <c>N</c> by <c>255*J - N</c> in the cell
      above and evaluate again to watch the second negative undo the first.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-images-transpose">
    <title>Transposition: Reflecting Across the Diagonal</title>

    <p>
      Transposition interchanges the roles of the row index and the column index, so the
      pixel in row <m>i</m> and column <m>j</m> of <m>M^T</m> is the pixel in row
      <m>j</m> and column <m>i</m> of <m>M</m>. Geometrically, the picture is reflected
      across the main diagonal, the line running from the upper-left corner to the
      lower-right corner. Note also that a tall <m>m \times n</m> image becomes a wide
      <m>n \times m</m> one.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-sca-transpose">
      <caption>
        The transpose <m>M^T</m>: the picture reflected across the diagonal running from
        its upper-left to its lower-right corner. The image is now as wide as the
        original was tall.
      </caption>
      <image source="images/sca-transpose.png" width="72%">
        <shortdescription>The grayscale illustration reflected across its main diagonal, so that it lies on its side and appears mirrored</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      A reflection is not a rotation, and it is easy to conflate the two. Turning the
      page to look at <xref ref="fig-sca-transpose" text="global" /> sideways does not
      recover the original picture: the reflected image is also mirrored. We will see in
      <xref ref="subsec-images-multiplication" text="title" /> that a genuine rotation
      requires a transposition <em>and</em> a flip.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        graphics_array([matrix_plot(D, cmap='gray', vmin=0, vmax=255),
                        matrix_plot(D.transpose(), cmap='gray', vmin=0, vmax=255)]).show()
      </input>
    </sage>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-images-multiplication">
    <title>Matrix Multiplication: Flips and Crops</title>

    <p>
      Multiplication is the operation whose visual meaning is least obvious, so we build
      it up from the identity matrix. Since <m>I_m M = M</m> and <m>M I_n = M</m>,
      multiplying by an identity matrix leaves the picture alone. Now permute the columns
      of the identity: let <m>K_n</m> be the <m>n \times n</m> matrix obtained from
      <m>I_n</m> by writing its columns in the reverse order,
      <md>
        K_4 = \begin{bmatrix}
          0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
          1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </md>
      Multiplying on the right by <m>K_n</m> reverses the order of the columns of
      <m>M</m>, and multiplying on the left by <m>K_m</m> reverses the order of its rows.
      In pictures, <m>M K_n</m> is the mirror image of <m>M</m> and <m>K_m M</m> is
      <m>M</m> turned upside down.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-images-noncommutative">
      <p>
        Here is <xref ref="sec-matrix-multiplication" text="title" /> made visible: the
        products <m>K M</m> and <m>M K</m> are genuinely different pictures, one flipped
        top to bottom and the other left to right. Multiplication does not commute, and
        which side you multiply on decides what happens.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <p>
      With both flips available we can say precisely what a rotation is. Reflecting
      across the diagonal and then reversing the order of the columns gives
      <md>
        M^T K_m,
      </md>
      which is <m>M</m> rotated a quarter turn clockwise. So a rotation is a
      transposition followed by a flip, exactly as promised in
      <xref ref="subsec-images-transpose" text="title" />.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-sca-flips">
      <caption>
        Two products of <m>M</m> with a reversal matrix. Multiplying on the right
        reverses the columns; multiplying on the left reverses the rows.
      </caption>
      <sidebyside widths="44% 44%" margins="auto">
        <figure xml:id="fig-sca-flip-lr">
          <caption>Mirrored: <m>M K_n</m>.</caption>
          <image source="images/sca-flip-lr.png">
            <shortdescription>The grayscale illustration mirrored left to right, with the cat now facing the other way</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-sca-flip-ud">
          <caption>Upside down: <m>K_m M</m>.</caption>
          <image source="images/sca-flip-ud.png">
            <shortdescription>The grayscale illustration turned upside down</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Cropping is the same idea with a non-square factor. Delete rows from <m>I_m</m> to
      leave a <m>k \times m</m> matrix <m>E</m>, keeping only the rows of the identity
      that correspond to the rows of the picture you want; then <m>EM</m> is the
      <m>k \times n</m> matrix consisting of just those rows. Doing the same on the right
      selects columns, so a rectangular crop is a product <m>EMF</m>. This is how the
      <m>80 \times 60</m> patch around the eye in
      <xref ref="subsec-images-grayscale" text="title" /> is cut out of <m>M</m>: by
      multiplication alone, with no need to speak of indices at all.
    </p>

    <p>
      The reversal matrix has a <m>1</m> exactly where <m>i + j = n - 1</m>, which is
      what the rule below builds. (Sage indexes rows and columns from zero.) Evaluate the
      cell and compare the four pictures: the original, the two flips, and the quarter
      turn.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        Km = matrix(ZZ, m, m, lambda i, j: 1 if i + j == m - 1 else 0)
        Kn = matrix(ZZ, n, n, lambda i, j: 1 if i + j == n - 1 else 0)
        pictures = [D, D*Kn, Km*D, D.transpose()*Km]
        graphics_array([matrix_plot(A, cmap='gray', vmin=0, vmax=255)
                        for A in pictures], nrows=1).show(figsize=8)
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      One more check is worth making. Reversing the columns twice should return the
      original picture, which says that <m>K_n K_n = I_n</m>. Sage agrees:
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        Kn*Kn == identity_matrix(ZZ, n)
      </input>
    </sage>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-images-inverse">
    <title>Inverses: Scrambling a Picture and Putting It Back</title>

    <p>
      The reversal matrix <m>K_n</m> is one example of a <term>permutation matrix</term>:
      a matrix obtained from an identity matrix by rearranging its rows. Every such
      matrix is a product of the row-swap elementary matrices of
      <xref ref="sec-elementary-matrices" text="title" />, and so is invertible. If
      <m>P</m> is an <m>m \times m</m> permutation matrix, then <m>PM</m> is the picture
      with its rows of pixels shuffled into a new order <mdash /> unrecognizable, if the
      shuffle is thorough.
    </p>

    <p>
      Because <m>P</m> is invertible, no information has been lost. By condition (7) of
      <xref ref="thm-invertible-matrix-theorem" text="type-global" />, each column of the
      scrambled picture came from exactly one column of the original, so the original can
      be recovered as
      <md>
        P^{-1}(PM) = (P^{-1}P)M = I_m M = M.
      </md>
      Better still, a permutation matrix satisfies <m>P^{-1} = P^T</m>, so the inverse
      costs nothing to write down: it is the transpose. Shuffling the rows and
      unshuffling them are the same operation performed with <m>P</m> and with
      <m>P^T</m>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-sca-scramble">
      <caption>
        A permutation matrix <m>P</m> shuffles the rows of pixels into an unrecognizable
        image (a). Since <m>P^{-1} = P^T</m>, multiplying by <m>P^T</m> restores the
        original exactly (b).
      </caption>
      <sidebyside widths="44% 44%" margins="auto">
        <figure xml:id="fig-sca-scrambled">
          <caption>Scrambled: <m>PM</m>.</caption>
          <image source="images/sca-scrambled.png">
            <shortdescription>Horizontal bands of gray in no discernible order, the rows of the illustration shuffled</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-sca-restored">
          <caption>Restored: <m>P^T(PM) = M</m>.</caption>
          <image source="images/sca-restored.png">
            <shortdescription>The original grayscale illustration, recovered exactly</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
    </figure>

    <p>
      In the cell below, <c>Permutations(m).random_element()</c> picks a shuffle of the
      <m>m</m> rows at random and <c>.to_matrix()</c> turns it into the corresponding
      permutation matrix. The last line checks that the transpose really is the inverse.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        P = Permutations(m).random_element().to_matrix()
        scrambled = P*D
        restored = P.transpose()*scrambled
        print(P.transpose() == P.inverse(), restored == D)
        graphics_array([matrix_plot(A, cmap='gray', vmin=0, vmax=255)
                        for A in [D, scrambled, restored]], nrows=1).show(figsize=6)
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      Both printed values should be <c>True</c>, and the third picture should be
      identical to the first. Run the cell a few times: the middle picture changes with
      every new random <m>P</m>, and the third never does.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-images-encryption">
      <p>
        It is tempting to call this encryption, and in a very weak sense it is: <m>P</m>
        is the key, <m>PM</m> is the ciphertext, and <m>P^T</m> is the decryption key.
        But no pixel has been changed, only moved, so the shuffled image has exactly the
        same collection of rows as the original and is easily unscrambled by anyone
        willing to match up the edges. The mathematics that matters here is not the
        secrecy: it is that an invertible matrix loses nothing, and a non-invertible one
        does. Multiplying by a matrix that is not invertible <mdash /> averaging
        neighbouring rows, say, or discarding every second one <mdash /> also produces a
        new picture, but there is then no matrix that undoes it.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>
</section>
